    description: "FOUND 2026-11-01 by the tracker-hygiene sweep (the investigator for WI-fovop; lab ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/tracker_hygiene_sweep_2026-11-01T223538Z). Re-reproduced at dev e362b4ba4f.\n\nMECHANISM. apex.py:876 declares file_patterns [\"*.cls\", \"*.trigger\"]; latex.py:402 declares [\"*.tex\", \"*.sty\", \"*.cls\"] (taxonomy.py:454 lists *.cls for latex). discovery.py has content classifiers only for .m, .d and .php, and nothing forbids two analyzers claiming one glob without a classifier.\n\nREPRO (~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/tracker_hygiene_sweep_2026-11-01T223538Z/unfiled_repros/fixtures/cls: Foo.cls = an Apex class, mycls.cls = a LaTeX class with \\ProvidesClass). analyze_apex reports files_analyzed=2 and analyze_latex reports files_analyzed=2, so each analyzer parses the other's file. In this fixture neither emitted a symbol from the foreign file.\n\nNOT MEASURED: whether either analyzer mints symbols, edges or partial-parse records from a foreign .cls on a real corpus (Salesforce repos; LaTeX class files).\n\nSAME MECHANISM as WI-fovop (.v: V and Verilog) and WI-namaj (.pp: Pascal and Puppet); sweep family F-shared-extension. Fix: a content classifier for .cls (a LaTeX class opens with \\NeedsTeXFormat or \\ProvidesClass; Apex opens with an access-modified class, interface or enum). Structural cure for the family: a registry-time check that any glob claimed by more than one analyzer has a registered classifier, which would catch .v, .pp and .cls together."  # 8e7c
